---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Quick Start">
  <h1>Quick Start</h1>
  <p>Run Identity locally against an EntDB container, then sign up a user and verify the access token.</p>

  <h2>1. Start EntDB</h2>
  <p>
    Identity needs an EntDB instance. The fastest way is the EntDB demo
    container (in-memory WAL, single tenant):
  </p>
  <Code code={`docker run -d --name entdb -p 50051:50051 ghcr.io/elloloop/entdb:latest`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>2. Start Identity</h2>
  <Code code={`docker run -d --name identity \\
  -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \\
  --link entdb \\
  -e GATEWAY_ENTDB_ADDRESS=entdb:50051 \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID=local \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=localhost \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=http://localhost \\
  -e GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=true \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:0.1.0`} lang="bash" title="docker run" />
  <p>
    With no <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code> set, Identity auto-generates a
    throwaway RSA key on startup and logs <code>auto_generating_dev_rsa_key</code>.
    See <a href="/identity/docs/installation/jwt-keys">JWT Keys</a> for production setup.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Sign up a user</h2>
  <p>
    Connect-RPC accepts JSON over HTTP. Each RPC is a POST to
    <code>/identity.IdentityService/&lt;Method&gt;</code> with the request
    message as the body and <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"correct horse battery"}'`} lang="bash" />
  <p>Response (truncated):</p>
  <Code code={`{
  "user": {
    "id": "user_...",
    "email": "alice@example.com",
    "status": "USER_STATUS_ACTIVE",
    "role": "admin"
  },
  "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...",
  "refreshToken": "rt_...",
  "expiresIn": 900
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>4. Call an authenticated RPC</h2>
  <Code code={`TOKEN=$(curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"correct horse battery"}' \\
  | jq -r .accessToken)

curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/GetCurrentUser \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{}'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>5. Verify the token externally</h2>
  <p>
    Downstream services should fetch the JWKS and verify access tokens
    locally rather than calling Identity on every request.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://localhost/.well-known/jwks.json`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {"kty":"RSA","use":"sig","alg":"RS256","kid":"dev","n":"...","e":"AQAB"}
  ]
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>6. Inspect metrics</h2>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://localhost:9090/metrics | head`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration">Configuration</a> — every environment variable</li>
    <li><a href="/identity/docs/installation/jwt-keys">JWT Keys</a> — production key ring</li>
    <li><a href="/identity/docs/auth/passkey">Passkey login</a></li>
    <li><a href="/identity/docs/auth/totp">TOTP (2FA)</a></li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
